<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Allostery | Yassir Boulaamane</title><link>https://yboulaamane.github.io/tags/allostery/</link><atom:link href="https://yboulaamane.github.io/tags/allostery/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><description>Allostery</description><generator>Hugo Blox Builder (https://hugoblox.com)</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><image><url>https://yboulaamane.github.io/media/icon_hu_4d696a8ace2a642b.png</url><title>Allostery</title><link>https://yboulaamane.github.io/tags/allostery/</link></image><item><title>A Protein Model Is a Sequence, a Fold, and a Surface</title><link>https://yboulaamane.github.io/blog/a-protein-model-is-a-sequence-a-fold-and-a-surface/</link><pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://yboulaamane.github.io/blog/a-protein-model-is-a-sequence-a-fold-and-a-surface/</guid><description>&lt;p>A protein in a calculation is three objects, and they are not interchangeable. The sequence is which residues are present, including the isoform and the protonation. The fold is the ensemble of shapes that sequence occupies. The surface is the patch a partner actually touches. What enters the coordinate file, what the force field is allowed to do to it, and which observable the trajectory is asked to produce are three different questions.&lt;/p>
