&lt;h2 id="twenty-residues-are-free-and-one-is-not">Twenty residues are free, and one is not&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>The standard genetic code builds proteins from twenty amino acids. Selenocysteine is inserted during translation where a UGA codon is recoded, not as a free choice at every position. Humans make it in a small set of selenoproteins, on the order of twenty-five. Pyrrolysine is not part of the human code. A rotamer library and a force field that offer selenocysteine at every site are describing a protein the cell does not make.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>Length is a separate choice. Fifty residues is a peptide or a small domain. A chain of a few hundred residues is an ordinary globular protein. The reviewed human titin entry UniProt Q8WZ42 is 34,350 residues, and other isoforms differ, so one gene is not one chain to drop into a box [1]. What can actually be solvated is a domain, or a few domains. Titin is hundreds of those, a spring in muscle, not a single globular fold.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>The sequence space of even one ordinary chain is already past any screen. For 375 positions and 20 residues at each position,&lt;/p>
$$
20^{375} \approx 7.7 \times 10^{487}.
$$&lt;p>That sits hundreds of orders of magnitude above the roughly $10^{80}$ atoms estimated for the observable universe. Almost every sequence is chemically writable and biologically unused. The gene is the filter. A docking screen or a generative model that samples protein-like strings without that filter is sampling chains no cell makes.&lt;/p>
