&lt;p>One crystal structure, or one minimized frame, is a sample. The
is the argument for watching the chain move, and for not reading a short trajectory as the ensemble. Protonation belongs in the same sentence as allostery. A change in the charge of a buried histidine can shift the same populations without any ligand at all.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="a-pathway-is-a-queue-of-surfaces">A pathway is a queue of surfaces&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>A pathway is many proteins doing one job: taking up a nutrient, running glycolysis, answering a xenobiotic. Each step is a surface finding the next surface. There is no controller in the middle. Binding and rates are the whole mechanism.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>The version a cytochrome P450 project actually computes is narrower. A ligand of the aryl hydrocarbon receptor binds the cytosolic receptor. The receptor changes partners and moves to the nucleus, binds DNA with ARNT, and one of the genes that comes on is CYP1A1. The enzyme can make the incoming molecule easier to clear. It can also make it more reactive. Which of those happens is a property of the substrate and of the reaction on that isoform, not a property of the gene having turned on. A model of the sensor, a model of the enzyme, and a model of clearance are three calculations. The
is the same warning from the data side: a clearance number mixes mechanisms, and a $K_i$ on one isoform does not.&lt;/p>
